# WebAssembly

This folder holds a working WebAssembly module and the code that calls it. It is a sample: replace it with your own, and this note with what yours does.

WebAssembly is how you use code written in Rust, C, Go or AssemblyScript from a plugin. Unlike a native Node binary it runs on **mobile** too, so it is the portable route — which is why it is worth a sample rather than a paragraph of instructions.

## What is here

| File | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `module.wasm` | The compiled module: 39 bytes, one export, `answer()`, returning `42`. |
| `module.wat` | The same module in WebAssembly's text format, so the binary is not opaque. Documentation only — nothing in the build reads it. |
| `answer.ts` | Loads the module and exposes `getWasmAnswer()`. Written against the bundler you chose. |

`src/plugin.ts` registers a **Sample WASM answer** command that calls `getWasmAnswer()` and shows the result. That call is not decoration: a bundler tree-shakes an export nothing uses, so without a real call site the module would be dropped from `main.js` and the whole WebAssembly integration would be dead with the build still green.

## Replacing it with your own

1. Build a `.wasm` and put it in this folder. With Rust: install [Rust](https://www.rust-lang.org/tools/install) and [wasm-pack](https://rustwasm.github.io/wasm-pack/installer/), `wasm-pack new my-wasm`, `wasm-pack build --target web`. With [AssemblyScript](https://www.assemblyscript.org/): `npm install assemblyscript`, then `npx asc assembly/index.ts --outFile src/wasm/module.wasm`.
2. Point `answer.ts` at it and name your own exports. `src/wasm.d.ts` declares what a `.wasm` import yields under this project's bundler — that differs between bundlers, which is why the file is generated rather than copied.
3. Keep something in `src/` calling into it, for the tree-shaking reason above.

## The one rule that is not obvious

Obsidian ships a plugin as `main.js` and `styles.css` and nothing else. A bundler that emits the module as a **separate** `.wasm` file next to the bundle produces a plugin that works in your repo and breaks the moment it is released — the file is simply not there. So the build is configured to **inline** the module into `main.js`, and every bundler needs telling that in its own way. If you swap bundlers, check that first.

## Provenance

`module.wasm` is the sample module from [CodeScript Toolkit](https://github.com/mnaoumov/obsidian-codescript-toolkit)'s demo vault, hand-written and 39 bytes long. `module.wat` is its source.
